1	//! The starting config: one template, every key commented out, and the commands that write it.
2	//!
3	//! The template is `iiac-perf.example.md`, compiled in, so the file in the repo and the file an
4	//! installed binary writes cannot differ. It holds every key at its built-in default, or at a
5	//! sample value where a key has none, each one commented out, so a fresh file changes nothing
6	//! until a line is uncommented.
7	//!
8	//! - `init-config [PATH]` prints the template, or writes it to PATH, never over a file. A
9	//!   `.toml` PATH gets headings and keys without the prose, which as comments buries the keys.
10	//! - `init-config --from OLD [PATH]` brings a file up to date by writing a fresh one: the
11	//!   template with every key OLD sets uncommented at OLD's value. A key OLD lacks arrives with
12	//!   the template, and a key the loader no longer knows fails OLD's parse by name. OLD is never
13	//!   written, and its author's own prose is what the new file loses.
14	//! - Run flags on an `init-config` line set their keys in the new file, so a command line that
15	//!   worked becomes a file. Under them is what that line would have run on: the run keys the
16	//!   host's XDG and local files set, layered as the loader layers them, or `--from`'s or
17	//!   `--config NAME`'s file in their place. So the file is the run the line makes here. The
18	//!   host's `[freq]` and `[profiles]` are not copied: a run under the new file still gets them
19	//!   from the host's files, and a copied clamp is wrong on the next host.
20	//! - `update-config FILE` is the same fill written back over FILE: its own values, the line's
21	//!   over them, the old file kept as `FILE.bak` only when `--backup` asks.
22	//! - `setup-freq` creates a missing XDG file from the same template, the live `[freq]` filled in.
23	//!
24	//! The template's one rule makes this mechanical: inside a `toml` fence a `#` with no space
25	//! after it is a commented-out key or table header, `#blocks = 100`, and one with a space is a
26	//! comment. A table has a fence to itself after every top-level key.
27	
